---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import PG14Notice from '../components/PG14Notice.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'PostgreSQL Upgrade Readiness Review',
  serviceType: 'PostgreSQL major-version upgrade readiness review',
  provider: { '@type': 'ProfessionalService', name: site.brand, url: site.url },
  areaServed: 'Worldwide',
  url: `${site.url}/postgresql-upgrade-readiness`,
  description: 'Fixed-scope assessment quoted after a fit review'
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="PostgreSQL Upgrade Readiness Review (PG14 EOL) — WalStack"
  schema={schema}
  description="PostgreSQL 14 upgrade readiness review for RDS, Aurora and self-managed: extensions, plan-regression risk, baseline and a validation plan, at a fixed price."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Upgrade track"
    title="PostgreSQL Upgrade Readiness Review"
    description="Identify blockers and performance-regression risks before a major-version change. Build a representative baseline, test critical plans and agree on go/no-go and rollback criteria."
    primaryHref="/contact?offer=upgrade&from=/postgresql-upgrade-readiness"
    secondaryLabel="See synthetic upgrade report"
    secondaryHref="/sample-upgrade-readiness-report"
  />
  <section><div class="container"><PG14Notice /></div></section>
  <section>
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Why the mechanics are not the hard part</h2>
      <p>On RDS and Aurora, a major-version upgrade or a blue/green switchover is a documented, mostly managed operation, and <code>pg_upgrade --check</code> catches many blockers on self-managed servers. What those tools do not tell you is how your own workload will behave on the new version: which plans change, which queries slow down until statistics are rebuilt, which extension or driver behaves differently, and how you would know within the first hour.</p>
      <p>This review is about that part: evidence from your workload before the change, a short list of what to compare after it, and clear go/no-go and rollback criteria.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid three">
        <div class="card"><span class="kicker">Inventory</span><h3>Version + extensions</h3><p>Identify extension and application dependencies that can affect the target-version path.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="kicker">Performance</span><h3>Baseline before change</h3><p>Capture representative workload behavior so regressions are observable rather than anecdotal.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="kicker">Validation</span><h3>Know what to compare</h3><p>Define critical queries, plans, latency and operational checks for the post-upgrade window.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Typical review areas</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Current engine and extension inventory</li>
        <li>Target-version choice and support horizon</li>
        <li>Application/ORM compatibility checklist</li>
        <li>Major plan-regression risks</li>
        <li>Staging/replay strategy</li>
        <li>Pre-upgrade workload baseline</li>
        <li>Post-upgrade validation and rollback considerations</li>
        <li>RDS/Aurora-specific path where relevant</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What you send</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Platform (RDS, Aurora or self-managed), current version and the target you are considering</li>
        <li>Extension list with versions: <code>SELECT extname, extversion FROM pg_extension;</code></li>
        <li>Output of the read-only <a href="/evidence-collection">evidence collector</a> as the pre-upgrade baseline</li>
        <li>Client drivers, ORM and connection pooler versions</li>
        <li>Any planned change window, replicas, logical replication or CDC consumers</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What you receive</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A written readiness report: blockers, risks ranked by likelihood and impact, and the evidence for each</li>
        <li>A recommended upgrade path and target version for your platform, with its trade-offs</li>
        <li>The critical queries and plans to compare before and after, plus the latency and error signals to watch</li>
        <li>Go/no-go and rollback criteria for the change window</li>
        <li>A review call with your team</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Price</h2>
      <p><strong>{site.upgradeOffer.display}</strong> for {site.upgradeOffer.scope}. Fee and delivery date depend on extension count, platform, baseline evidence and availability; both are agreed before work starts. See <a href="/pricing">how quoting works</a>.</p>
      <p><a class="button primary" data-track="primary_cta_click" href="/contact?offer=upgrade&amp;from=/postgresql-upgrade-readiness">Ask about upgrade readiness</a></p>
      <p>Review a <a href="/sample-upgrade-readiness-report">synthetic readiness report</a> based on the reproducible self-managed upgrade lab.</p>
      <p>To run the checks yourself first, use the <a href="/postgresql-14-upgrade-checklist">PostgreSQL 14 upgrade checklist</a>. For the most common post-upgrade slowdown, see <a href="/insights/postgresql-slow-after-major-upgrade">why PostgreSQL can be slow after a major upgrade</a>.</p>
      <p class="notice"><strong>Important:</strong> I do not execute the production upgrade as part of this review unless separately scoped. Your team or managed-service provider owns the change window.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section><div class="container"><CTA eyebrow="Upgrade readiness" title="Planning a major PostgreSQL upgrade?" description="Start with your version, platform and extension list. The first step is scoping the smallest useful readiness review." primaryHref="/contact?offer=upgrade&from=/postgresql-upgrade-readiness" secondaryLabel="See synthetic upgrade report" secondaryHref="/sample-upgrade-readiness-report" /></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
